-----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----

"Phil G. Fraering" <pgf@srl01.cacs.usl.edu> said :

> General Magic's developers used RSA's BSAFETM Cryptography
> Toolkit to provide Telescript with advanced encryption and digital
> signature features based on the patented RSA Public Key CryptosystemTM
> and RSA's RC4TM symmetric stream cipher, the fastest encryption scheme
> commercially available

Unfortunately, as far as I'm aware RC4 only has a 40-bit key (compared
to 56 for DES or 128 for IDEA), and is therefore easy for an agency
like the NSA (or probably even a bunch of crackers/hackers with access
to lots of machines) to break by brute-force methods. I think they're
also only using 512-bit RSA keys, which again are thought to be fairly
easy for the NSA or similar groups to break (but probably still beyond
what crackers could do).

I guess it's better than nothing (or the Wiretap chip for that matter),
but I wouldn't trust it with anything secure - once your RSA key is
broken everything you send (or sent previously if it's been captured
and stored) using it can be decrypted at will. I definitely wouldn't
call it advanced when compared to PGP, or even RIPEM.

			Mark


