Reilly Jones misses the point:
>Eric Forst wrote 3/25/94: <If we find that two beliefs of which we are certain
>lead to a logical contradiction, we reject the belief of which we are less
>certain.>
>
>This continuous, sloppy mixing of terminology is wearing thin.  You are certain
>of knowledge, facts, truth.  You are uncertain of beliefs.  Beliefs are
>relative.  Certainty is absolute, "free from doubt."  There is simply no such
>thing as "less certain."  You are either certain or you are uncertain.  You can
>be more uncertain or less uncertain.  Mere repetition of your position of "we
>reject the belief of which we are less certain" over and over and over does not
>increase it's intelligibility, it merely indicates fuzzy, wooden-headed,
>relativistic argumentation.

Reilly, you're stuck in abstractions. You're using words as pigeon holes,
and not everything fits into the structure you've devised.

The difference between facts and beliefs is entirely subjective. Something
I am certain is a fact today, can reveal itself to have been an incorrectly
held belief tomorrow. PCR recognizes this phenomenon and attempts to give
me a tool for dealing with it, by redefining certainty to be non-binary and
multi-variant. The binary definition of certainty is a crude and inadequate
tool with insufficient flexibility.

This redefinition is similar to what you've done to create your own
structure. You have redefined "weight", "consensual", created the domains
of "knowledge and meaning" vs. "purpose and value" and whatnot.
Unfortunately, this framework seems to have been labelled as a Truth. It is
a concept that is beyond question and, sadly, a cracked lens that distorts
reality enough to make it difficult for you to discuss it with others.

To quote Python, "It's only a model."

This "lens" makes paragraphs like the following rational to you:

>Objective reality is inaccessible to us due to epistemological boundaries.  We
>approach it through science within consensual reality, the domain of knowledge
>and meaning.  Subjective reality is completely accessible to us and us alone,
>the domain of purpose and value.  These distinctions are very real, they are
>aspects of the only existence there is, not subsets.

Because I don't share your redefinitions and framework, this looks like
you're ontologically biting your own tail. Aspects and not Subsets? Its
seems like a spurious distinction to me.

I have only one comment on your consensual reality theory: I didn't consent
to it! We are obviously living in massively different first person
subjective realities.

