 Overview and Vision: What is CommerceNet?
 =========================================

 Imagine having instant access to detailed product information from
 all potential suppliers of a critical component -- right from your
 desktop. Imagine the benefits of being able to deliver information
 about your products to buyers throughout Silicon Valley -- by simply
 posting it on your computer system.

 Imagine ordering that critical component by simply making a  
selection from
 your computer screen. Or securing an order from a new customer who  
found
 you by searching an electronic database. 


 Now, imagine sharing design and data files with a workgroup that  
extends
 beyond your department -- beyond even your company's remote sites --  
to
 include everyone you need for effective collaboration on a critical  
project. 


 You've just imagined CommerceNet, the real world implementation of  
the
 electronic information infrastructure in Northern California. 


 CommerceNet: an open, Internet-based infrastructure for electronic  
commerce.
 Created and developed by a coalition of Silicon Valley  
organizations,
 CommerceNet pioneers the deployment of a public information network
 designed to enhance communication and productivity. It is designed  
to provide
 widespread high quality, low cost network network applications to  
businesses
 and organizations throughout Northern California. 


 CommerceNet provides services and applications designed to make the  
Internet
 easy to use and suitable for commercial use. CommerceNet defines  
protocols
 and standards to enable commercial development of new services  
available to
 any individual or organization in the region. 


 CommerceNet creates an ``industrial strength'' Internet that  
provides the
 reliability and security required for business with the ease of use
 that will make it accessible to even novice and occasional users. 


 What does CommerceNet Provide?
 ==============================

 Specifically, CommerceNet provides: 


  o Services -- Basic enabling services required by virtually every  
user and
    application: generic directories, secure multimedia messaging,  
network
    access control, and payment facilities. 

  o Applications -- A framework for the development of compelling
    applications and services targeted to electronic commerce among
    companies in the region. Applications designed to address the  
varied
    business and community needs of CommerceNet users will be  
developed
    by third party providers in compliance with protocols established  
by
    CommerceNet. 

  o Connectivity -- High quality, affordable Internet access with  
minimal
    on-site equipment or network expertise required. 

  o Outreach -- In order to deliver the benefits of electronics  
commerce to
    as wide an audience as possible CommerceNet will aggressively
    promote awareness and use of public networks throughout the  
region it
    serves. 


 The Internet: Access to a World of Possibilities
 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++

 The Internet is the world's largest confederation of public and
 private computer networks. Since its evolution from a Department of
 Defense network used by academic and defense researchers, the  
Internet
 has exploded in size and scope. More than 15,000 networks are
 accessible via the Internet with additional networks joining on a
 daily basis.

 Because it is built on the Internet, CommerceNet makes regional
 product and service providers accessible to anyone in the world with
 an Internet connection. A manufacturer posting product  
specifications
 via CommerceNet makes those specifications available to a huge  
number
 of potential buyers, reaching an audience that was previously  
inaccessible. 


 Principal Users
 +++++++++++++++

 CommerceNet is open to any organization in the region offering or  
seeking
 on-line information or services. Initial participants include  
companies in
 Silicon Valley's electronics food chain: computer system  
manufacturers,
 subsystem suppliers, service providers and semiconductor  
manufacturers. 


 CommerceNet anticipates that routine use by hundreds of  
organizations will
 lead to the development of a wide variety of applications -- many of  
them
 completely unanticipated. 


 By minimizing the cost and complexity typically associated with  
proprietary,
 non-participatory networks, CommerceNet actively promotes widespread
 participation by small and mid-sized companies. Technical  
assistance, software
 toolkits and training materials are available for both users and  
service
 providers. 


 Putting Electronic Commerce to Work
 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++

 Open to any interested user or provider of on-line services,  
CommerceNet is an
 easy to use and widely available on-line marketplace. In contrast to
 conventional on-line information services, CommerceNet is designed  
to create
 a participatory, ``many-to-many'' market for services, information  
and dialog.
 There are as many uses for CommerceNet as there are providers and  
users. 


 Information and service providers can use CommerceNet toolkits -- or  
contract
 with a third-party service -- to convert existing applications into  
network
 services supporting CommerceNet's access, authentication and  
remittance
 protocols. Entrepreneurs and small businesses can use CommerceNet  
toolkits to
 build new information services. 


 Providers maintain full local control of their data because it runs
 on their own servers. Providers can make available virtually any
 information or service desired by a potential user -- product data
 sheets, catalogs, training videos, design files, order forms,  
enhanced
 directories, etc.

 An easy-to-use start-up kit allows users to plug into CommerceNet  
via the
 Internet. Once on the network, users will navigate an integrated web  
of
 multi-media information and services using intuitive interfaces and  
electronic
 forms. 


 Third party directory, brokering and advertising services help users  
locate
 services both regionally and nationwide. Users have seamless access  
to
 information that is traditionally dispersed over a variety of  
sources, such as
 telephone directories, classified advertisements and catalogs. 


 CommerceNet is composed of a Core Development Team (Enterprise
 Integration Technologies (EIT), Stanford University Center for  
Information
 Technology and the Western Research and Educational Network  
Corporation
 (WestRen), operators of the Bay Area Regional Research Network  
(BARRNet))
 and a large number of corporate participants. Sponsoring  
organizations are
 Smart Valley, Incorporated, Joint Venture: Silicon Valley and the  
State of
 California's Office of Strategic Technology. 


 The CommerceNet Consortium is a non-profit corporation operating  
under a
 matching funds grant from the United States government's Technology
 Reinvestment Program (TRP). The TRP is sponsored by the Advanced  
Research
 Projects Agency (ARPA) of the Department of Defense, the National  
Institute
 of Standards and Technology (NIST) and the National Science  
Foundation
 (NSF). 


